Short:        One pack: AmigaOS 3.2.3, ready to use
Uploader:     (the Team fills this in at upload time)
Author:       AmigaChrome Team
Type:         util/misc
Version:      0.8.9
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.2.0
Distribution: Aminet

OpenUp sets up an AmigaOS 3.2.3 installation in one go. Install AmigaOS 3.2.3,
run OpenUp, reboot: graphics on every monitor, networking, MUI programs,
printing, mail, archives and file types all work, with nothing more to read.

It finds what it is running on (a real Amiga with its cards, a PiStorm, or
AmigaChrome) and installs the Open family to match. Anything it replaces is
kept, and one uninstaller puts everything back.

Version 0.7 is for AmigaChrome instances. Its Installer installs, as you
choose:
- OpenSocket: the network, through the x86 or ARM64 cores (bsdsocket.library
  4.3);
- OpenPrint and OpenView: printing to PDF and network printers, and viewing; OpenView takes
  MultiView's place, so whatever opened MultiView opens OpenView (MultiView is kept);
- AmigaChrome sharing: the clipboard and files, shared in and out of the
  Amiga, and an optional Send Out icon to drop files on;
- Backgrounds: a theme's desktop as Workbench's backdrop, for your screen
  (Open, Glass, Graphite, Ember, Clear or Sky). Since 0.8.2 that is all OpenUp
  changes of Workbench's look: the rest is the OS's own, or the OpenKick ROM's,
  and an upgrade takes out the look files an earlier OpenUp wrote (OpenLook
  and its theme) where you haven't changed them. Your Font and IControl prefs
  stay as they are (0.8.7);
- OpenTypes: which program opens each kind of file (SYS:Preferences/File Types);
- OpenCompress: opens, tests and extracts ADF, ADZ, DMS, LhA, zip, tar and
  gzip, from its window in SYS:Utilities or from the Shell;
- OpenService: services from AmigaChrome's boards and from a Cradle you pair
  with (DEVS:openservice.device, and C:Nursery to list them); nothing goes on
  the network until a Cradle is paired.
- OpenImage: datatypes for PNG and Targa (TGA) pictures (the Team's own decoders),
  WebP and WebM on the Amiga itself, and through
  OpenService for AVIF, HEIC, JPEG XL, camera RAW, SVG, FLAC, Ogg, MP3, MIDI,
  MP4 and other video, and office documents, and music modules: MOD, MED,
  Oktalyzer, DigiBooster, XM, S3M, IT, played on the Amiga itself through
  AHI, with the mixing on another core when there is one;
  datatypes already there stay;
- OpenWindows: windows snap to edges and to each other, Amiga+Tab switches
  between them, a double-click in a title bar brings the window to the
  front, the wheel scrolls the window under the pointer, and programs'
  windows open where they were last left, Workbench drawers too if you like
  (Windows in SYS:Preferences);
- OpenDock: a dock for your programs, starting with ToolManager's, AmiDock's
  or AmiStart's buttons when you have one (Dock in SYS:Preferences);
- OpenUp Setup: a few questions at the next start, and the startup switches;
- OpenMenus: menus in the theme's look, from the screen bar or at the
  pointer, and a menu for each icon and for the desktop on the right button,
  with MagicMenu's settings taken over; the bar can sit at any edge of the
  screen, and the menus work from the keyboard;
- OpenFiles (new): a file manager with two panes and
  tabs, a bar of drive buttons, safe copies, a trashcan on each disk and undo
  (SYS:Utilities);
- OpenPlay (new): a media player for everything the datatypes open, with a
  playlist and a slideshow (SYS:Utilities);
- OpenTitle (new): AmigaChrome's logo, the free chip and fast memory, a
  clock, and a cog at the right end that opens any settings editor, on the
  title bar, set in SYS:Preferences/Title Bar; beside the cog, the taskspace holds
  other programs' icons (Tata's first);
- Sound (new): the volume, Amiga sound (Paula) and AHI sound in one editor,
  and a speaker on the menu bar (SYS:Preferences/Sound; the OS's own Sound
  editor stays where it is); it also carries OpenAHI, the Team's own AHI for AmigaChrome's
  ACAHI sound board: ahi.device, the board's driver acaudio.audio and its modes
  in DEVS:, and AHI's settings on the Host mix mode (kept if you have your own).
  OpenAHI is MIT licensed and has no Aminet code in it, and it goes only onto
  AmigaChrome machines, never onto a real Amiga or a PiStorm;
- OpenEdit (new): a text editor with tabs, find and replace, and colours
  for scripts and code (C:OpenEdit);
- OpenBlanker (new, always installed): the screen blanker; after five minutes
  without a key or the mouse it plays Gods and Angels, AmigaChrome's homage
  to the people who made the Amiga and kept it alive
  (SYS:Tools/Commodities/OpenBlanker; ENV:OpenPrefs/Blanker sets the seconds);
- The Shell's font (new, always installed): DejaVu Sans Mono in FONTS:, 8
  to 24 pixels high, fixed width, for Font in SYS:Prefs ("System default
  text"); the OpenKick ROM's own Shell font is its 11, and OpenUp changes no
  Font prefs;
- The Shell icon (new, always): a Shell from SYS:System/Shell opens 80
  columns by 24 rows, as NewShell's own window, not 130 pixels high (its
  WINDOW tool type loses the 130; OpenUpTool REMOVE Parts/ShellIcon puts it
  back);
- OpenFont (new, always installed on a 68040 or 68060 with its FPU):
  openfont.library and DejaVu Sans, the font OpenBench's labels and the Open
  programs draw their text in (without them, the screen's font);
- OpenWrite (new): a word processor for DOCX, ODT and Amiga documents,
  with PDF export, printing, spelling and an ARexx port
  (SYS:Utilities/OpenWrite), and C:OWConvert for scripts;
- OpenMail (new, a preview, off unless picked): a mail reader for IMAP mail
  over TLS, set up from the address for Gmail, Outlook and others
  (SYS:Utilities/OpenMail); it reaches mail servers through OpenTLS,
  which comes with it;
- OpenBrowser Lite 0.4.1 (new): a small web browser, about 100 KB and no
  WebKit (SYS:Utilities/OpenBrowserLite): pages laid out with headings,
  lists, links, pictures through datatypes and their CSS, no JavaScript;
  https:// through OpenTLS, which comes with it. Ticked on a 68040 or 68060
  with its FPU, which it needs;
- OpenBrowser (new, a preview, off unless picked): a web browser built on
  WebKit, in its own archive, OpenUp-<version>-OpenBrowser.lha, unpacked
  beside the pack; it needs an FPU and is best on a 68040;
- OpenMulticore (new): openmulticore.library, so programs can hand work to
  the other cores of AmigaChrome's cores board (music modules in OpenPlay
  do), or to the main CPU where there is no board; OMCTest checks it and
  OMCBench measures it (SYS:Tools);
- OpenMUI (preview, off unless picked): zunemaster.library, MUI built from
  AROS's Zune for OS 3.x, beside MUI, with ZuneTest (SYS:Tools). Where
  LIBS:muimaster.library is absent it also installs a muimaster.library
  (version 19, MUI 3.8's) that passes every call to Zune, so MUI programs
  run; a MUI you installed is never touched, and the uninstaller removes the
  face;
- OpenRTG (new, off unless picked; ticked on the OpenKick ROM with an ACRTG
  board, since 0.8.7): Workbench on OpenRTG's own 16-bit screen
  on AmigaChrome's graphics board, without Picasso96, whose monitor for that
  board is put aside and comes back with the uninstaller. It brings
  openrtg.library 0.19.1, whose Native modes follow the size of AmigaChrome's
  window, with opengpu.library 0.16 and the board's driver; an upgrade brings
  it up to date where it is installed and keeps the screen mode you chose.
  Since 0.8.2 OpenUp writes a screen mode only when a new install asks and you
  say Yes (on the OpenKick ROM Workbench is on that screen by itself);
- OpenGPU (new): opengpu.library 0.15, so programs' drawing and SDL 2 games
  run on the x86 or ARM64 cores and GL (Mesa) on the GPU, through
  AmigaChrome's graphics board, and on the Amiga's own CPU where there is no
  board; OpenGfx, the faster graphics.library drawing, is inside it now
  (opengfx.library 1.2 stays for programs that open it); SDL 2 (with
  SDL2_image and SDL2_mixer) and GL are modules in LIBS:OpenGPU, loaded
  once, kept resident and shared by every program that asks; TinyGL
  programs run on GL too (LIBS:tinygl.library), and C:OpenGPUCheck checks
  it;
- Warp3D and MiniGL (new): Warp3D.library 5.0 and minigl.library 29
  (OpenGL 1.1) on OpenGPU, for Warp3D and MiniGL games and programs; they
  need a 68040 or 68060 with its FPU; a Warp3D.library already there
  (Wazp3D's) is kept, and Wazp3D's settings are taken over (C:Warp3DPrefs
  changes them; W3DTest and MGLTest in SYS:Tools show them working); on a
  real Amiga with a graphics card's own Warp3D (LIBS:Warp3D/HWdrivers) the
  part is left out, so the card keeps drawing;
- Icons: OpenIcons, the Team's own icon set (look B, the look of the OpenKick
  ROM's own icons), as new pictures for the system's own drawers, programs
  and file types' icons (0.8.4, in place of Gorilla), and OpenUp's own
  drawers drawn the same way (OpenIcons 0.2); the old pictures are
  kept and come back with the uninstaller. Not the default icons (RAM, hard
  disk, floppy, drawer, tool, project, trashcan...) or SYS:'s own disk icon:
  those are the ROM's own, and an upgrade takes out OS 3.2.3's and the ones
  an earlier OpenUp gave, unless you changed them;
- OpenCrypto (new): opencrypto.library 2.0, the sums behind secure
  connections (SSH's key exchanges, host keys, SHA-2, AES), on the x86 or
  ARM64 cores on AmigaChrome and on the Amiga's own CPU elsewhere;
- OpenTLS (new, off unless picked; it comes with OpenFTP, OpenMail and
  OpenAmigaPython): opentls.library,
  secure connections (TLS 1.2) for the Open programs, OpenFTP's FTPS first,
  with its sums through OpenCrypto; the server's certificate is checked
  against Mozilla's list of certificate authorities (S:OpenTLS, MPL-2.0,
  its licence beside it; your own go in ENVARC:OpenTLS/certs);
- OpenPuTTY (new, off unless picked): PuTTY 0.85 for AmigaOS 3.2 on
  OpenCrypto, with SSH, Telnet and serial terminals and key sign-in
  (SYS:Utilities/OpenPuTTY); it brings OpenCrypto with it and needs a
  network stack;
- OpenDemos (new, off unless picked): Boing, Chrome Gears and Copper Tunnel,
  three small 3D demos in GL on OpenGPU (SYS:Utilities/OpenDemos);
- Tata (new, off unless picked while it is 0.x): before a program runs or
  a file opens, Tata checks it against a list of programs and files known
  to crash, draw wrongly or fail on AmigaOS 3.2.3 or with OpenUp
  (S:Tata.known), and asks first; its window is SYS:Prefs/Tata, its icon
  sits on the title bar, and "Tata OFF" in a Shell turns it off;
- OpenFTP (new, off unless picked): FTP and FTPS in two panes, with a site
  manager (SYS:Utilities/OpenFTP); plain FTP works as it is; FTPS works
  through OpenTLS (opentls.library), which comes with it;
- Unofficial Telegram for Amiga (Open) (new, a preview, off unless picked):
  Telegram chats in a window (SYS:Utilities/UnofficialTelegram); it isn't
  made by Telegram, and it needs setting up first: your own Telegram API key
  (free, from my.telegram.org) and Telegram's service on AmigaChrome's x86 or
  ARM64 cores or a paired Cradle; until then its window says what is missing;
- OpenGPU developer files (new, off unless picked): SDL 2's, SDL 1.2's,
  OpenGL's and GLA's headers and link libraries, with examples, in
  SYS:Developer/OpenGPU-SDK (assign OpenGPU-SDK:), for building programs on
  the Amiga;
- OpenInput (new, off unless picked): openinput.library, game controllers
  for every program in one standard layout, with C:OIList, and Gamepads in
  SYS:Prefs to test and map pads and choose which drives which port (an
  opt-in patch there lets older games see a modern pad); on AmigaChrome it
  sees the Amiga's ports for now;
- OpenDrawer (new, off unless picked): drawer windows with an icon line,
  icons or a list sorted by name, size, date or kind, search and Snapshot,
  in the look's theme, beside Workbench (SYS:Utilities/OpenDrawer; drawers
  dropped on its icon open in its windows);
- OpenBench (new, a preview, off unless picked): the Team's own Workbench,
  with the dock and the Open preference editors built in, in SYS:System; it
  runs where the Startup-Sequence starts SYS:System/OpenBench in place of
  LoadWB, as AmigaChrome's own instances do (OpenUp doesn't change the
  Startup-Sequence); on AmigaChrome it follows the window, and Resize to
  Window fits Workbench to it;
- OpenDateTime (new): the date, the time and the region in one editor
  (SYS:Preferences/Date & Time); you pick a place, not an offset, and summer time
  follows by itself;
- OpenAmigaPython (new, off unless picked): Python 2.7.18 for AmigaOS 3,
  OpenAmigaPython 2.7.18-ac3, in SYS:Tools/OpenAmigaPython, with Python:
  assigned and on the Shell's path from the next start; ssl and https work
  through OpenTLS, which comes with it, and sockets need a network stack
  (OpenSocket, Roadshow or AmiTCP); any Amiga with a 68020 or later, no FPU
  needed;
- OpenLibs (new, each off unless picked): the Team's own rewrites of
  AmigaOS libraries in place of OS 3.2's, one switch each on the Installer's
  sixth page, offered only once each one's test against OS 3.2's own, call
  for call, is clean: utility.library 47.100 (a 68020 or later),
  mathieeedoubbas.library 47.100 (a 68040 or 68060 with its FPU),
  input.device 50.2 (a 68020 or later) and OpenRexx 48, ARexx's
  rexxsyslib.library and rexxsupport.library (a 68040 or 68060 with its
  FPU; OS 3.2's RexxMast and Rexxc commands work with them). The ROM ones are loaded by OS 3.2's
  own LoadModule, so the Amiga restarts once by itself as it starts;
  unticking one puts OS 3.2's own back. On the OpenKick ROM, which has its
  own, those two are left out (0.8.7), and an S:Pre-LoadModule-Startup an
  earlier OpenUp wrote there is taken out.
OpenPrefs, every settings editor of ours in one window, has a drawer of its
own, SYS:Preferences: the program, and an icon for each page by its plain
name (Theme, Screen Mode, Fonts, Sound, Date & Time, File Types...). The
drawer is on the Shell's path, so "OpenPrefs SOUND" works there. The old
editors' names in SYS:Prefs, SYS:Prefs/OpenPrefs and C:OpenPrefs go to
SYS:Storage/OpenUp-Superseded. SYS:Prefs keeps the OS's own (Env-Archive,
Presets, Classic), OpenTata and OpenUp-Setup. On the open ROM, which applies
Sound's levels, the gamepads and the stack caps itself at boot, User-Startup
no longer runs OpenPrefs APPLY; under OS 3.2.3's own ROM it does.
Workbench's Tools menu gets two sub-menus (new): OpenUp, with the Open
programs that are installed (and OpenUp Update...), and Preferences, with
their settings editors (Theme, Dock, Sound, Date & Time, Input, Gamepads,
Serial, Printers, Network, File Types, Tata, OpenUp Setup). SYS:C/OpenUpMenu keeps them there; "OpenUpMenu
QUIT" takes them out.
OpenSnap (new, always installed; SYS:Tools/Commodities/OpenSnap, started at
boot) takes pictures of the screen. Shift + Right Amiga + S takes the whole
front screen, Shift + Right Amiga + W the active window (its frame too).
Each goes to SYS:Snapshots as a dated PNG (Snapshot-2026-10-24-15.42.07.png,
a window's with its title after the time) and onto the clipboard as an IFF
picture, so Right Amiga + V pastes it into a paint program. A short message
on the title bar says where it went. RTG screens of any depth and the
Amiga's own screens (AGA, HAM and Extra Half-Brite too) all work. The PNG
needs OpenImage (openpng.datatype 47.4 and z.library); without it the
picture is saved as IFF. The keys, the drawer and the clipboard are its
icon's tool types (SCREENKEY, WINDOWKEY, DRAWER, CLIPBOARD), and Exchange
lists it.
From a PC, in an AmigaChrome instance's window: the right Windows key and
the Menu key are both Right Amiga. On Windows, Win + Shift + S is the
Snipping Tool's, and on Ubuntu (GNOME), Super + Shift + S is the screenshot
tool's, so Windows or GNOME takes that one before the browser sees it: use
the Menu key there (Shift + Menu + S, Shift + Menu + W). On a PC without a
Menu key, give a key the Right Amiga in Cradle's keyboard mapper, or give
OpenSnap other keys in its icon's tool types. On a Mac, Cmd + Shift + W
closes the browser window, so set WINDOWKEY to another key there.
OpenMonitor (new, SYS:Utilities) shows the tasks, the CPU and the cores,
memory and the GPU in the theme's font, and ends a program from its
right-click menu. On the open ROM it is the ROM's and OpenUp puts only its
icon there.
LIBS:softfpu.library (new, with OpenUp itself): the FPU in software for a
68LC040 or 68EC040 under the OpenKick ROM, which opens it at boot; with an
FPU, or under OS 3.2's own ROM, nothing opens it.
Every file it adds or replaces is listed in ENVARC:OpenAmiga/OpenUp.manifest;
replaced files are kept in SYS:Storage/OpenUp-Superseded; its startup lines
sit in one marked block in S:User-Startup. Uninstall-OpenUp (in
SYS:Utilities/OpenUp) takes it all out again.

